\section*{Method}
Put
\[
d=12x+7.
\]
Then the equation is equivalent to
\[
d\mid 21n^3+19,
\qquad
k=\frac{21n^3+19}{d}.
\]
If \(7\mid d\), then no solution is possible, since
\[
21n^3+19\equiv 5\pmod 7.
\]
For \(d=12x+7\) with \(7\nmid d\), one has \(\gcd(21,d)=1\), and hence
\[
21n^3+19\equiv 0\pmod d
\]
is equivalent to
\[
n^3\equiv -19\cdot 21^{-1}\pmod d.
\]
Thus each root \(r\) of this cubic congruence gives the infinite arithmetic progression
\[
n\equiv r\pmod d.
\]
The first member of that progression satisfying \(n\geq N=10^{20}\) is
\[
n=N+a_0,
\qquad
 a_0\equiv r-N\pmod d,
\qquad
0\leq a_0<d.
\]
Then all corresponding solutions are
\[
\boxed{
 n=N+a_0+dt,
 \quad x=\frac{d-7}{12},
 \quad k=\frac{21(N+a_0+dt)^3+19}{d},
 \quad t=0,1,2,\ldots
}
\]
Every row of Table \ref{tab:rootclasses} is one or more such progressions.

